WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.640
Director David Gordon Green's Halloween, the
reboot-sequel that directly connects to John

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.640
Carpenter's original film and retcons the
many less-awesome follow-ups that came after

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.100
it, featured an ending that finally gave Laurie
Strode, played by Jamie Lee Curtis, the closure

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.080
she'd sought for 40 years, while keeping Michael
Myers' fate relatively open for interpretation.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.060
But Halloween almost concluded on much different
note.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:25.230
Obviously if you've come this far in this
video you should know: spoilers for the 2018

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.180
Halloween ahead.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.400
By the end of the film, Laurie, her daughter
Karen, and her granddaughter Allyson trapped

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.140
Michael inside Laurie's basement, then set
the entire house ablaze.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.260
It'd have been safe to assume Michael would
be burnt to a crisp until the film cut back

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.190
to the flame-filled basement that now appeared
to be Michael-free.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.680
Fans who stayed through the credits heard
his breathing just before the lights went

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.059
up, which further signaled that he may have
made it out of the fire alive.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.180
But an early version of the film saw things
play out differently.

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:56.520
In a May 2017 script draft posted to Twitter,
Laurie, Allyson, and Karen originally named

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:00.730
"Shanah" come together outside Laurie's house,
where Laurie engages in a one-on-one battle

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.220
with Michael.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.260
Rather than a game of cat and mouse that ends
up with Michael locked in the basement, the

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.670
alternate ending sees Laurie and Michael getting
into a knife-fight outside.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.120
Laurie's luck against Michael runs out when
he drives a knife into her chest.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.500
She swings her own knife and slices his arm.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.280
Here's where Karen/"Shanah" enters the scene,
slowly opening the front door, armed with

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.530
"a high tech crossbow."

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:24.360
After Michael twists the knife further, "forces
[Laurie] to the ground," and then "pulls the

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.220
blade out," Laurie's daughter shoots Michael
in the chest — at least that's what we can

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.040
assume happened.

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:32.760
There's a page missing from the Halloween
script that was shared on social media.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.060
Next we see Laurie's daughter and granddaughter
flagging down a pick-up truck, loading Laurie

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.250
in it, and pulling away with the unnamed driver
seemingly in search of a hospital, somewhat

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.929
similarly to the way the final cut of the
film ends.

00:01:43.929 --> 00:01:47.999
The script then cuts to a dark forest, where
Michael staggers through the trees as he yanks

00:01:47.999 --> 00:01:49.270
an arrow out of his chest.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.689
He ends up "standing among the shattered artificial
faces of mannequins" — likely a reference

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:57.470
to the area of Laurie's property where she
and her daughter held target practice, which

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:58.720
we saw in the film.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:03.140
He then walks to a tree and sits down, touches
the wounds in his chest and looks at his missing

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.729
fingers, all while breathing laboriously.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:09.330
As the script notes, he's exhausted, and possibly
taking his final breath.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:13.860
Compared to how Halloween actually ended — Laurie,
Karen, and Allyson having survived and only

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.200
Michael's ultimate fate left ambiguous — this
original conclusion would have featured Laurie

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.820
and Michael both on the brink of death.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.780
Pretty thrilling, and more than enough room
for a sequel, right?

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.410
So why didn't they go with this version?

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.420
Two words: test audiences.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.780
The filmmakers held reshoots after test audiences
made it known that they didn't like the ending

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:31.420
very much.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.640
And as cool as a knife-fight and crossbow
scene would've been, it's hard not to agree.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:40.120
Sure, the original ending sounds pretty solid
in most regards, but it was probably a good

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.010
thing that it was changed for the final cut.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.340
Imagine it: Laurie anxiously awaiting Michael's
return for 40 years, training and raising

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.570
her daughter to fight and kill him, to the
point where the authorities had to take her

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:53.140
away — only to be stabbed and left bleeding
in the back of a pick-up truck?

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.960
That would've been a massive bummer.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.960
With critics and fans having highly praised
Halloween, and the flick raking in hundreds

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.030
of millions of dollars at the global box office,
it's all but inevitable that we haven't seen

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.700
the last of Michael, Laurie, Karen, and Allyson.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.510
Maybe Laurie will still get to have that knife
fight, and Karen will hoist the crossbow for

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.180
another moment of utter badassery, in the
possible sequel?

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.940
A horror fan can only hope.

